title A fourth first-principles proposal: row-aligned large Tally gardens for energy
prompt Continue the DIFFUSER lane's standing brief -- generate moonshot research aimed at Caravan, Aurora, Tally, and Mantra, argued from first principles, Gauge Field discipline, bounded claims and a stated falsifier.
think Ran fleet_round_open.sh; HEAD settled at e0e176a28e, no contested rebase, three dead-letter session logs and 22 dead-letter files reported and left alone (not this lap's to touch).
think Read the live ITINERARY DIFFUSER blocks: the torus/radial search across Caravan, Aurora, Comlink, Tablecloth, and Mantra closed negative this week, and the prior lap already handed Bakery three buildable proposals from that thread. Continuing torus/radial would repeat closed ground.
think Chose a new first-principles axis named in the brief but not yet worked this week: energy-saving compute via memory-access locality, aimed at Tally specifically since it is the allocator every other seated module borrows.
think Grounded the claim in two cited, public facts (Horowitz ISSCC 2014 DRAM-vs-compute energy figures; DRAM row-activation dominance, standard controller behavior) before reading tally/region.rye and tally/gardens.rye to check what Tally's bump allocator already does.
think Found the honest first result was not a proposal at all -- Tally's bump allocation is already the energy-favorable access pattern, arriving from TAME's bounded-allocation discipline rather than by intent. Named that plainly rather than manufacturing a bigger ask.
think Scoped the one real gap (garden start-address alignment to a DRAM row) tightly: it only matters for gardens large relative to a row, explicitly excluding tally/seed.rye's own 64-byte worked example rather than overclaiming across every garden.
think Checked this sandbox for perf and /sys/class/powercap/intel-rapl -- both absent -- and wrote the falsifier as unmeasured-here rather than implying a result that was never run.
think Graded the paper at Field setting: first pass composite 73/C (register 49, driven by 22 negative sentences out of 43; reach 30, driven by a Flesch-Kincaid grade of 18 against an 11 ceiling). Per quality-assurance.md, a sub-B grade pushes a bounded repair frame rather than shipping as-is.
think Rewrote flagged negative sentences into affirmative phrasing without dropping any figure or claim, then split long em-dash-joined sentences into shorter ones to bring the reading grade down. Re-graded: register 90, reach 100, composite 91/A.
think Per the-writer-sheds, shelved my prior live DIFFUSER account (the three Bakery-buildable torus proposals) whole via tools/i/itinerary_account_shelf.sh rather than stacking a fourth live block on the card, then wrote the new account as a two-line pointer to the fresh paper.
think Wrote the commit body twice -- the commit-msg hook refused the first draft for carrying only 2 of the required 3 mechanism words; the second draft named Region.init, parameter, and file explicitly and passed.
think Rewrote construction/ITINERARY.md's Git nib to HEAD's parent per remember-git-nib.md rule 2, staged it, and amended it into the same commit since no further rebase was pending this lap.
rota lap 5941, row 1 mod 5 -- Fire, the row that sees. Read the threshold page (cardinal Standfast, fixed reds-first, dual the three depths of removal). It named the shape of what I did without prompting it: no open red touched my seam so no line needed stopping, and the elder DIFFUSER account was shelved rather than deleted or silently overwritten -- a molt-depth cut, walk-back intact, matching the page's own lesson that a cut is finished only when it is made at exactly the depth the truth requires.
obs The torus/radial thread being closed negative is itself useful signal for a research lane: it means the crux this week is finding a genuinely new axis rather than deepening an exhausted one. Memory-access energy is that axis, and it produces a proposal small enough for Bakery to build without waiting on hardware.
obs Grading below B on a first draft is normal for dense first-principles prose; the repair (affirmative phrasing plus shorter sentences) cost about fifteen minutes and lifted the letter grade two full steps without touching a single cited figure.
